Employee security training:
  A strong security implementation is up-to-date and accurate employee training. By giving employees the best possible information, they become aware of current security
  information, as well as the business's incident response plan. These make it much easier for employees to make better cybersecurity decisions, including how to spot phishing
  emails, what makes a strong password, what constitutes suspicious behaviour. Better trained employees reduce the risk of data breaches, and helps reduce the severity of
  data breaches.
Zero trust network segmentation:
  Zero trust network segmentation is when a network always treats all users, devices and applications as untrusted. This is done through a couple of ways. The first is that
  every access request must be authenticated, no matter where it came from. The second is least privileged access, which is where devices or users always have the minimum user
  permissions that are required to do the job they're assigned. The third is to assume that a breach is inevitable, forcing security teams to design controls to contain threats
  that have already infiltrated the system. These are all strong security implementations because they remove any assumptions that could prove to be incorrect and potentially 
  harmful for the business.
Data encryption and backup:
  It is a strong security implementation to encrypt a company's data, as well as storing a recent backup of that data. This makes it much harder for attackers to: 1. access
  the potentially sensitive data, and 2. the data cannot be used against the company in situations where ransomware has infected the company. If a company is infected with 
  ransomware, provided the backup is stored in a different location, they don't have to worry about paying the hackers, because they still have access to the ransomed data.
Access control systems:
  A strong security implementation is strong access control measures. Good access control methods ensure that a company's operating data, and physical devices/ records can
  only be accessed by the people authorised to use them. For example, having different keys for higher-clearance level rooms, using unique passwords/ PINs to only grant access 
  to places the person is permitted to be in. From a cyber point-of-view, access control systems prevent groups of people accessing documents through user permissions. Only people
  who can access/ edit a file can do so.
Security cameras:
  Security cameras are important security implementations. They provide a deterrent for attackers, and provide a log of activities in the case of an incident. Without
  security cameras, it becomes much harder for a business to seek reimbursement for physical losses, and much harder for perpetrators to be caught.

